BT 1 0 0 1 0 123.3 Tm /F1 8.7 Tf 12 TL (The recommended platform uses a hash table for student, course and job identifier lookup, a min-heap for background) Tj T* (scheduling, and different sorting algorithms according to the report requirement. A well-distributed hash function with a) Tj T* -0.000954 Tw (controlled load factor provides expected O\(1\) exact lookup, while collision handling and resizing protect correctness as records) Tj T* 0 Tw (grow. The min-heap orders jobs by \(priority, arrival sequence\), giving O\(1\) access to the next job and O\(log n\) insertion and) Tj T* -0.000345 Tw (removal; this preserves urgency and fairness for equal priorities. For large score reports that must retain the order of equal-key) Tj T* 0 Tw (records, stable merge sort is preferred because it guarantees O\(n log n\), at the cost of O\(n\) additional memory. For fixed-width) Tj T* (non-negative activity IDs, radix sort can be faster when the digit range is known. Heap sort is a suitable memory-constrained) Tj T* (alternative because it guarantees O\(n log n\) time with O\(1\) auxiliary space, but it is not stable. CPU threading, MapReduce or) Tj T* (CUDA should be introduced only after profiling shows that sorting cost justifies synchronization, communication or transfer) Tj T* (overhead. A limitation is the extra memory required by stable merge sorting. A future improvement is adaptive partitioning and) Tj T* (benchmarking on the target production hardware.) Tj T* ET
